---
import CourseLesson from "../../../layouts/CourseLesson.astro";
import Math from "../../../components/course/Math.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/course/Callout.astro";
import Quiz from "../../../components/course/Quiz.astro";
import BasisGallery from "../../../components/course/widgets/BasisGallery.astro";
---

<CourseLesson lessonSlug="haar-transform-and-review">
  <h2>The Haar transform: rectangles with a shrinking footprint</h2>
  <p>
    Like Walsh–Hadamard, the <strong>Haar transform</strong> is built from rectangular ±-valued basis functions
    — but it's generated by a different rule, one indexed by a scale <Math tex="r" /> and a position <Math
      tex="m"
    /> rather than a single index:
  </p>
  <Math
    tex="HA(r,m,x) = \begin{cases} 2^{r/2}/\sqrt{N} & \frac{m-1}{2^r} \le x < \frac{m-\frac12}{2^r} \\[4pt] -2^{r/2}/\sqrt{N} & \frac{m-\frac12}{2^r} \le x < \frac{m}{2^r} \\[4pt] 0 & \text{elsewhere} \end{cases}"
    display
  />
  <p>
    <Math tex="r" /> sets the scale — how wide the function's support is — and <Math tex="m" /> slides that support
    along the interval. As <Math tex="r" /> grows, <Math tex="2^r" /> shrinks the active window, so higher-scale
    basis functions cover a smaller and smaller slice of the domain instead of oscillating across the whole thing.
  </p>

  <h2>Basis images</h2>
  <BasisGallery kernel="haar" size={8} label="8 × 8 Haar basis images" />

  <h2>What makes Haar different: locality</h2>
  <p>
    Look closely at the grid above next to the Hadamard grid from the last lesson. Every Hadamard basis image
    has structure spread across the <em>entire</em> tile — sign changes reach from edge to edge. Most Haar basis
    images, by contrast, are flat gray almost everywhere, with all the "action" compressed into one small block.
  </p>
  <Callout variant="intuition" label="Why this matters">
    <p>
      A transform whose basis functions are spread across the whole image (Fourier, DCT, Hadamard) is good at
      describing <em>global</em> patterns — overall brightness gradients, repeating textures. A transform whose
      basis functions are concentrated in small regions is good at describing <em>local</em> ones — a sharp edge
      in one corner, a small bright spot — without smearing that information across every coefficient. That locality
      is exactly the property later generalized into <strong>wavelets</strong>, which is why the Haar
      transform is usually taught as "the simplest wavelet transform" rather than just another basis swap.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Review: four transforms, one formula</h2>
  <p>
    Every transform in this unit is an instance of the same idea from two lessons ago — <Math tex="T = KfK" /> for
    some orthonormal kernel <Math tex="K" /> — just with a different family of basis vectors packed into <Math
      tex="K"
    />:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fourier</strong> — complex exponentials; smooth, global, but complex-valued.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Discrete Cosine</strong> — real cosines; smooth, global, and strongly energy-compacting (JPEG).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Walsh–Hadamard</strong> — real ±1 rectangles; global, cheap to compute, ranked by sequency.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Haar</strong> — real ±1 rectangles that shrink in support with scale; local, the ancestor of wavelets.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Same input image, four different lenses — each one making a different kind of structure easy to see, and
    easy to throw away without losing much.
  </p>

  <Quiz
    question="An algorithm needs to detect a small, sharp defect in one corner of an image without disturbing the representation of the rest of the image. Which transform's basis functions are best suited to that, and why?"
    options={[
      "The DFT, because complex exponentials are precise",
      "The DCT, because it's used in JPEG",
      "The Haar transform, because its basis functions are localized rather than spread across the whole image",
      "They are all equally suited, since all four are orthonormal",
    ]}
    correctIndex={2}
    correctText="Right — orthonormality guarantees a valid basis, but only Haar's basis functions are concentrated in small regions, so a local feature shows up in a few coefficients instead of being smeared across all of them."
    incorrectText="Orthonormality alone doesn't imply locality. Fourier, DCT, and Hadamard basis functions all span the entire image; only Haar's basis functions shrink to a small support, which is what makes it good at isolating local features."
  />

  <Quiz
    question="Which property specifically explains why JPEG can discard most DCT coefficients with little visible loss?"
    options={[
      "The DCT basis functions are rectangular",
      "The DCT concentrates most of a typical image block's energy into a handful of low-frequency coefficients",
      "The DCT is a local transform like Haar",
      "The DCT is faster to compute than addition-only transforms",
    ]}
    correctIndex={1}
    correctText="Exactly — energy compaction, not speed or locality, is the property compression relies on."
    incorrectText="Speed and rectangular basis functions describe Hadamard, not DCT — and DCT is a global transform, not a local one. The property that matters here is energy compaction: most of the signal ends up concentrated in a few coefficients."
  />
</CourseLesson>
